  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 115 PM CST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nA cold upper low is spinning near Lake Erie this afternoon, with\nembedded shortwave troughs rotating around it. Surface high\npressure centered over Hudson Bay has its axis extending all the\nway south through the Great Plains. Northerly flow between this\nhigh and the low to the east has kept a cold wind blowing today,\nbringing the coldest day of the season so far. Although some sun\nthis morning led to widespread stratocumulus this afternoon, we\nexpect much of this to dissipate this evening. The result will\nbe a clearing sky with winds diminishing as the surface high's\ninfluence becomes stronger. Radiational cooling will lead to\nwidespread low temperatures below freezing, with the coldest\ntemperatures across the western forecast area where winds will\nbe lightest and the sky will be clearest. The upper low\ncontinues moving northeast while the surface high gradually\nexpands its influence along the Mississippi River on Saturday.\nWith more sun, most areas reach 50 degrees. Saturday night, \nthough, will be another cold one with light winds and a clear \nsky bringing a hard freeze just about everywhere. However, some\nincreasing high level clouds from the west could act to hold\ntemperatures up a bit from what they otherwise would be.\n\nAs the upper low spins further away into the North Atlantic, \nupper ridging builds in its place while surface high pressure \ncontinues to dominate in the low levels. The result will be an \nair mass gradually modifying in place with temperatures nudging \nup a couple of degrees each day. This trend continues through at\nleast midweek when all guidance finally pushes the ridge \neastward and opens up a southwesterly flow over the central US. \nShortwave troughs passing through this flow and increased\nmoisture have the potential to bring a round or two of showers \nlater in the week. Among the 12Z LREF guidance, up to 40 percent\nof members bring measurable rain to parts of the forecast area \nby Thursday evening.",
